{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":276629,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"8eb81fb7-bea7-4cec-94c3-b76c28990d9f","total_cost_usd":1.2348694999999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":66926,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":20409,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":66926,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":20409,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":66926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":66926},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":46786,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.046891,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":20409,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":66926,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.1879784999999998,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"playtester\",\n  \"verdict\": \"targets_missed\",\n  \"summary\": \"Flown, this configuration never wants to aerobrake — a single committed pass is the cheapest and the coolest descent in all nine band/load cases, 104 measured pass-count points, with no U-curve anywhere — and the return from the high band costs less plate than the return from suborbital, so both of the risks §4.5 names as fatal are live rather than theoretical.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ablation_optimum_is_one_pass_everywhere\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"game_params.ablation.cost_curve asserts minima at 0/1/2 skims (suborbital [14.03, 14.60, 18.71, 27.40], low [24.68, 21.62, 23.56, 31.63], high [57.37, 43.12, 38.41, 44.58]), and GDD 2.3.1 requires that 'the cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes, never a single plunge and never feathering'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"cheapest_pass_count = 1 in all nine band x load descents. Ablation is monotonically increasing with pass count in every one of the 104 measured points: suborbital full hold 23.5 -> 28.0 -> 31.0 -> 33.8 -> 36.4 -> 39.0 -> 41.7%; low full hold 21.2 -> 65.7% over 14 pass counts; high full hold 19.3 -> 83.0% over 16. Peak heat also rises with pass count rather than falling (suborbital full hold 98 -> 101 -> 102 -> 102), so extra passes cost plate and heat at once. There is no local minimum above one pass anywhere in the sweep. The single lever that could create one — skim cooling — is measured at 0.956/0.912/0.867 for the low band and 0.971/0.944/0.918 for the high band, i.e. 2.9-13% off peak heat, against a fatigue toll that grows 2.6 -> 6.50 -> 12.35 -> 21.13. The saving can never exceed the toll.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"difficulty_falls_with_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.3.7 and 4.5 risk 2: 'the value curve and the risk curve rise together'; game_params.ablation.heat_index asserts 76/100/146, a 1.92x spread from suborbital to high.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The return leg gets easier as the band gets higher. Cheapest ablation, empty: suborbital 12.1%, low 11.0%, high 10.2%. Full hold: 23.5%, 21.2%, 19.3%. Peak heat, empty: 70, 67, 64. Full hold: 98, 93, 89. Every figure runs the wrong way. The exploration grid satisfies difficulty_rises_with_band in 43.5% of 324 worlds, so worlds where it holds are common — but this one is not among them at the cargo density the simulator flies.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"unstaged_shallow_pass_not_survivable_from_suborbital\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.3.1 makes pre-stage coarse braking the intended descent — 'shallow passes with the naked hull soaking slow heat, while thrust remains to raise or lower the periapsis between passes' — and states 'shallow unstaged passes are survivable because cargo damage only begins at 100% heat'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"unstaged_braking: suborbital shallow pass peaks at 407 on a 100-point bar against heat_capacity 100 — 4.1x over, survivable false. The low band peaks at 93 and survives with 7 points of margin. Unstaged beta is 685.7 kg/m2 against 228.6 staged, so the hull penetrates deeper before it brakes, and from the suborbital band the apoapsis (102.5 km) sits only 24.5 km above atmosphere_top (78 km), so the ship cannot climb clear between passes. The exploration grid satisfies unstaged_pass_survivable in 13.3% of worlds and in zero of the twelve highest-scoring configs — every 7/8 world in the grid fails on exactly this target, with unstaged peak heat 352-462. This is the target the design space resists hardest.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"bands_are_not_gated_by_the_tank\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.3.7: 'Reaching a band takes tank and thruster upgrades.' balance_notes assert the base ship reaches suborbital only, fuel_tank t1 (1900 kg) gates the low band and t2 (2400 kg) gates the high band.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The base 1400 kg tank reaches all three bands: suborbital with 135.8 kg remaining (9.7%), low with 83.91 kg (6.0%), high with 44.55 kg (3.2%). Fuel burned is 1264.2 / 1316.1 / 1355.5 kg — the entire suborbital-to-high spread is 91.3 kg, 6.5% of the base tank. No tank size can sit between two bands with a usable reserve on the lower one, because the planet's circular speed falls with altitude (842 / 772 / 690 m/s) and going higher barely costs more. The upgrade tree's first gate does not exist as designed, and the GDD's stated alternative gate — 'surviving the return from it takes heat shield upgrades' — is also absent per the inverted difficulty finding.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"cost_curve_overstates_plate_cost_by_up_to_5.6x\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.cost_curve puts a direct high-band entry at 57.37% of plate, a direct low-band entry at 24.68%, and the high band's optimum (2 skims) at 38.41%. balance_notes conclude from this that the high band 'is unflyable at base gear and needs heat_shield t1'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Measured plate cost for the same descents: high band empty direct 10.2% (claimed 57.37, a 5.6x overstatement), low band empty direct 11.0% (claimed 24.68, 2.2x), suborbital empty direct 12.1% (claimed 14.03, the only one close). Even a full hold from the high band costs 19.3%, half the claimed empty figure. plate_survives is true in every one of the nine descents. Replating at 3 per percent therefore bills 31-71 per run, not the 42-172 the curve implies, so the 'clean flying is cheaper' pressure is roughly a third of what the economy was balanced against.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"skim_cooling_overstated_and_band_dependent\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier is a single global array [1, 0.84, 0.72, 0.68] — a 16% cut in peak heat for the first skim, 32% by the third — and every entry in cost_curve and optimal_skims is computed from it.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Measured multipliers are both smaller and band-dependent: suborbital [1, 0.898, 0.730, 0.730], low [1, 0.956, 0.912, 0.867], high [1, 0.971, 0.944, 0.918]. The first skim buys 10.2% at suborbital, 4.4% at low and 2.9% at high — the band where the design most needs skimming to matter is the band where it does least. Cooling also saturates: suborbital is flat from the second skim on, and skim_benefit_saturates holds in 92.0% of the 324-world grid, so no world in the space rewards more than about two skims. Depth is the real lever the sweep confirms: a high-band direct entry peaks at 157.1 from the atmosphere top and 100.6 when committed 39 km deeper, a 1.56x swing from depth alone against 1.09x from three skims.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"only_the_single_pass_stays_under_the_cargo_damage_line\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"reentry.heat_capacity 100 is the line at which cargo damage begins, one slot per 3 s.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Full-hold descents peak at 98 (suborbital), 93 (low) and 89 (high) on one pass, and cross 100 as soon as a second pass is added: suborbital 101/102/102, low 99/100/101/101/102/102/103, high 95/98/99/101/100/101/101/103. So the only descent that avoids cargo damage at full hold is the one the plate cost already favours. Anti-feathering is over-delivered — skimming is punished twice, in plate and in heat, and there is no state in the sweep where a player benefits from it.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mass_lever_holds_but_cargo_is_11_pct_lighter_than_priced\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"balance_notes fix the full hold at 1149.9 kg (191.65 kg per crushed slot) and a 1.958x loaded mass ratio, and derive descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.3 from a 550 m2 canopy.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The simulator flies 170.89 kg per slot, a 1025.3 kg full hold and a 1.85x mass ratio, and touches down at 4.18 m/s full, 3.67 m/s half, 3.07 m/s empty. The claim survives — 4.18 against 4.3 is 2.8% and still 0.82 m/s under the 5 m/s soft line, and the sqrt-of-mass shape is exactly as 2.3.2 states — but the cargo density it is computed from is 11% light, so the margin quoted in balance_notes is optimistic by about that much. The greed pressure itself is real and measured: a full hold nearly doubles plate cost at every band (suborbital 12.1 -> 23.5%, low 11.0 -> 21.2%, high 10.2 -> 19.3%) and raises peak heat 25-28 points. greed_costs_something holds in 89.2% of the grid; full_hold_lands_soft in only 22.2%, so this margin is worth watching even though it currently holds.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"high_band_fuel_margin_will_not_pay_for_skimming\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"claim\": \"The descent model assumes the player has thrust in hand to shape periapsis between skims and then commit; balance_notes budget t2 reaching the high band with 609 kg spare.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The base ship arrives at the high band with 44.55 kg (3.2%), about 67 m/s at loaded mass. Measured commit burns between the skim periapsis and the committed entry are 46-48 m/s from the high band and 51-53 m/s from low, before the deorbit burn that started the descent. A one-skim high-band descent is therefore fuel-marginal as well as plate-expensive on the base tank. fuel_margin_sane is satisfied by only 34.9% of the grid, the third-tightest target in the space.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"proposed_changes\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"baseline.reentry.reference_area_m2\",\n      \"current\": 3.5,\n      \"proposed\": 7,\n      \"reason\": \"Halves staged ballistic coefficient from 228.6 to 114.3 kg/m2 and unstaged from 685.7 to 342.9 — the only quantity that makes a skim bleed enough speed to cool the committed entry, and the same move that brings the unstaged shallow pass down; it costs ascent drag on a high-band margin already at 3.2%, and invalidates baseline derivation steps 8-11, which must be recomputed against 1200/2350 kg at Cd*A = 10.5 m2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"baseline.planet.scale_height_m\",\n      \"current\": 6000,\n      \"proposed\": 4500,\n      \"reason\": \"Compresses the air column so shallow and deep are further apart in density, which is the lever the sweep measures at 1.56x against skimming's 1.09x, and lifts the suborbital band's clearance above a 13-scale-height atmosphere from 24.5 km to 43.5 km so an unstaged ship can climb clear between passes; it costs the Researcher's R_specific*T = 24000 derivation (becomes 18000) and moves every drag and aerobrake figure in baseline.json.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.cycle_toll_growth\",\n      \"current\": 1.5,\n      \"proposed\": 1,\n      \"reason\": \"The geometric toll alone (2.6, 6.50, 12.35, 21.13) exceeds every skim saving the simulator measures, so it guarantees the monotone curve found in all 104 points; a flat per-pass toll is the fallback GDD 4.5 risk 1 names for itself, and it costs the U-curve's elegance rather than its anti-feathering effect.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.max_passes\",\n      \"current\": null,\n      \"proposed\": 4,\n      \"reason\": \"The other half of the 4.5 risk 1 fallback — with a flat toll, feathering has to be capped by rule instead of by cost, since the sweep shows 13 to 22 passes are physically reachable at every band; it costs a new schema field and a Coder-side hard stop.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier\",\n      \"current\": [1, 0.84, 0.72, 0.68],\n      \"proposed\": [1, 0.96, 0.91, 0.87],\n      \"reason\": \"Replaces assumed cooling with the low-band measured series so cost_curve and optimal_skims are computed from what the ship actually does; it costs the claimed high-band two-skim optimum, which the measurement shows was never there, and the field should become per-band because measured cooling runs 0.898 at suborbital against 0.971 at high.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.heat_index\",\n      \"current\": {\"suborbital\": 76, \"low\": 100, \"high\": 146},\n      \"proposed\": {\"suborbital\": 79, \"low\": 100, \"high\": 124},\n      \"reason\": \"Measured direct-entry peaks at a common entry depth are 100.0 / 126.6 / 157.1, i.e. 0.79 / 1.00 / 1.24 against the low band; it costs the 1.92x band spread the balancer derived from catalog cargo density, and cost_curve must be recomputed from the corrected index and multipliers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.optimal_skims\",\n      \"current\": {\"suborbital\": 0, \"low\": 1, \"high\": 2},\n      \"proposed\": {\"suborbital\": 0, \"low\": 0, \"high\": 0},\n      \"reason\": \"Records what is flown today rather than what was modelled — one committed pass is cheapest in all nine cases; this entry should be re-measured, not kept, once the reference-area and scale-height changes above are in, since their whole purpose is to move it back to 1-2.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence_notes\": [\n    \"The simulator contradicts itself between its two descent models, and this is the single largest caveat on the band findings. In the skims table an empty direct entry peaks at 100.0 (suborbital), 126.6 (low) and 157.1 (high) — the high band 1.57x hotter. In the descents table the same empty direct entries peak at 70, 67 and 64 — the high band 0.91x, i.e. cooler. Both are labelled empty-ship single-pass. The descents table lets the solver choose braking altitude per band and the skims table fixes entry depth, which is probably the difference, but they cannot both be describing the same flight. Read difficulty_falls_with_band and heat_index as bounded by this: at a fixed entry depth the high band is hotter as designed; when the solver is free to choose depth, it is not.\",\n    \"The simulator flies one cargo density — 170.89 kg per slot — for all three bands. The balancer's entire band differentiation rests on catalog densities of 145.5 / 246.0 / 500.7 kg per crushed slot, a 3.4x gradient the sweep does not model. So the measured inversion is partly an artifact of equal-mass hauls. The finding that survives it is narrower and still load-bearing: at equal mass the high band is not harder to come home from, which means the whole difficulty curve is carried by debris_catalog.json alone, with no help from altitude, and the catalog is not in this sweep.\",\n    \"The exploration grid does not score the 2-4 pass optimum directly. Its nearest proxies are skimming_cools_the_entry (46.3% of 324 worlds) and skim_benefit_saturates (92.0%). So the grid cannot settle 4.5 risk 1 outright — but 92% saturation is itself evidence that no world in the space rewards more than about two skims, which is why I have called the 2-4 pass rule a design finding and reached for the GDD's own fallback rather than proposing a number for it.\",\n    \"The twelve best-scoring worlds in the grid are all radius 60 km (one at 160 km) with staged ballistic coefficients of 11-111 kg/m2. Adopting one is a full rescale of the planet, not a tune, and several sit at or below the 50 kg/m2 floor where the first contact with air removes the whole orbit. I have proposed the smallest moves toward that region — frontal area and scale height — rather than the destination.\",\n    \"Whether the reference-area and scale-height changes actually rescue the unstaged suborbital pass is a prediction, not a measurement. 407 on a 100-point bar is a 4.1x overshoot and reentry.unstaged_heat_multiplier is only 3, so even zeroing the multiplier leaves 136. The proposal must be re-flown before anyone believes it; if it does not close, GDD 2.3.1's pre-stage coarse braking phase is unavailable from the suborbital band and the rule needs rewriting rather than retuning.\",\n    \"This is a point mass with a perfect pilot. There is no attitude error, so off_retrograde_penalty of 1 is untested; no debris collisions; no chute deploy timing and no plasma window. Nothing here says whether the plasma-to-chute gap is tense or tedious, whether a 4.18 m/s touchdown reads as soft in the hands, or whether one committed pass is more or less frightening than three. Pillar three is entirely outside what this sweep can see.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":195056,"ttft_stream_ms":1691,"time_to_request_ms":188,"type":"result","duration_ms":274238,"uuid":"74fa81fa-8666-4f82-b0fe-448d1773d825"}
